      "label": "Enterprise-to-equity bridge",
      "classes": "cream revision-slide",
      "body": "<div class=\"header-row\"><h2>Calculate FCFF, then move from business value to equity value</h2></div><div class=\"rule\"></div><div class=\"fcff-reminder\"><strong>FCFF = cash available to lenders and shareholders</strong><code>EBIT × (1 − tax rate) + depreciation − capex − increase in operating working capital</code><p>Subtract investment needs. Do not subtract interest or debt repayments.</p></div><div class=\"bridge-equation\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Illustrative annual FCFF of 52 million held constant forever divided by 9 percent WACC gives 577.78 million enterprise value, less 300 million debt plus 45 million cash gives 322.78 million equity value, or 6.46 per share\"><div><span>Constant annual FCFF / WACC</span><strong>$577.78M</strong><small>$52M / 9%</small></div><b>− $300M debt<br>+ $45M cash</b><div><span>Equity value</span><strong>$322.78M</strong><small>Divide by 50M existing shares</small></div><b>→</b><div class=\"highlight\"><span>Value per share</span><strong>$6.46</strong><small>No-growth illustration</small></div></div><div class=\"deliverable\">Here, FCFF stays $52M each year forever. Growth comes later; the Apex workbook uses a separate 4% growth assumption.</div><div class=\"visual-caption\">FCFE already gives equity value. Do not subtract debt a second time.</div>",
      "note": "Remind students that FCFF begins with after-tax operating profit: EBIT times one minus the tax rate, plus depreciation and other noncash operating charges, less capital expenditures and increases in operating working capital. A decrease in working capital releases cash. Interest and debt repayments are financing flows and are not subtracted from FCFF. The supplied 52 million cash flow cannot be derived from EBIT alone; the example does not supply depreciation, capex and working-capital components. To introduce the enterprise-to-equity bridge without using an unexplained growth term, hold annual FCFF constant at 52 million forever, with 9 percent WACC. This deliberate no-growth illustration differs from the maintained Apex workbook, which assumes 4 percent growth. EV is 577.777778 million; less 300 million debt plus 45 million nonoperating cash yields 322.777778 million equity; divide by 50 million shares gives 6.455556 dollars. Do not mistake this instructional assumption change for a revised workbook answer: workbook growth case still yields 1,040 million EV, 785 million equity and 15.70 dollars per share. After the growth introduction students can revisit the effect of growth on value. Time: 3 minutes."
