      "classes": "cream excel-slide compact-payoff-slide",
      "body": "<div class=\"header-row\"><h2>Excel discounts both Year 1 cash flows together</h2></div><div class=\"rule\"></div>\n<div class=\"excel-sheet\"><div class=\"excel-ribbon\"><span>BUS311_EQUITY_VALUE.xlsx</span><b>Home</b><b>Formulas</b><strong>One-period value</strong></div><div class=\"formula-row\"><span>B7</span><b>ƒx</b><code>=-PV(B5,1,0,B3+B4)</code></div><div class=\"excel-grid\"><span></span><b>A</b><b>B</b><b>C</b><strong>3</strong><label>Dividend in Year 1</label><data class=\"input\">$3.00</data><small>Received at Year 1</small><strong>4</strong><label>Expected price in Year 1</label><data class=\"input\">$81.00</data><small>Received at Year 1</small><strong>5</strong><label>Required return</label><data class=\"input\">12.0%</data><small>Discount rate</small><strong>6</strong><i></i><i></i><i></i><strong>7</strong><label>Intrinsic value today</label><data class=\"result\">$75.00</data><small>=-PV(B5,1,0,B3+B4)</small></div></div>\n<div class=\"excel-payoff-note\"><strong>Why B3 + B4?</strong><p>The $3 dividend and $81 sale proceeds arrive together in Year 1. Discount their combined $84 once.</p><code>($3 + $81) / 1.12 = $75.00</code></div>",
      "note": "Connect the formula directly to the timing mistake on the preceding activity. B3 and B4 both arrive at Year 1, so combine them as the future value in PV. B5 is the required return. The leading negative sign displays a positive present value under Excel cash-flow sign conventions. Ask students to explain why adding an undiscounted three-dollar dividend to the discounted sale price is incorrect. Keep this explanation brief and move to the growing dividend stream. Time: 1 minutes."
