      "classes": "cream revision-slide sensitivity-excel-slide",
      "body": "<div class=\"header-row\"><h2>Excel shows why higher growth moves value more</h2></div><div class=\"rule\"></div><div class=\"excel-sheet sensitivity-answer-sheet\"><div class=\"excel-ribbon\"><span>GORDON_GROWTH.xlsx</span><b>Formulas</b><strong>Sensitivity comparison</strong></div><div class=\"formula-row\"><span>B5</span><b>ƒx</b><code>=B2/(B3-B4)</code></div><table class=\"sensitivity-worksheet\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>A</th><th>B · Base</th><th>C · Higher growth</th><th>D · Higher return</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>2</th><td>Next dividend D₁</td><td>$2.12</td><td>$2.12</td><td>$2.12</td></tr><tr><th>3</th><td>Required return r</td><td>8.5%</td><td>8.5%</td><td>9.5%</td></tr><tr><th>4</th><td>Dividend growth g</td><td>4.0%</td><td>5.0%</td><td>4.0%</td></tr><tr class=\"answer\"><th>5</th><td>Value today P₀</td><td>$47.11<code>=B2/(B3-B4)</code></td><td>$60.57<code>=C2/(C3-C4)</code></td><td>$38.55<code>=D2/(D3-D4)</code></td></tr><tr><th>6</th><td>Change vs. base</td><td>—</td><td>+$13.46<code>=C5-$B$5</code></td><td>−$8.56<code>=D5-$B$5</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"visual-caption\">Growth +1 percentage point narrows r − g to 3.5 points. Return +1 point widens it to 5.5 points.</div><div class=\"sensitivity-lab\" data-interactive=\"sensitivity\"><div class=\"sliders\"><label>Required return <input id=\"required-return-slider\" type=\"range\" min=\"6.5\" max=\"12\" step=\"0.1\" value=\"8.5\"><output id=\"required-return-value\">8.5%</output></label><label>Perpetual growth <input id=\"growth-slider\" type=\"range\" min=\"1\" max=\"6\" step=\"0.1\" value=\"4\"><output id=\"growth-value\">4.0%</output></label></div><div class=\"lab-result\"><span>Modeled value</span><strong id=\"gordon-output\">$47.11</strong><small id=\"spread-output\">r − g = 4.5 percentage points</small></div></div>\n      ",
      "note": "Reveal 60.57 dollars for growth at 5 percent and 38.55 dollars for required return at 9.5 percent. The spread moves from 4.5 points to 3.5 or 5.5. Use the sliders to show nonlinear sensitivity. Ask whether either teaching assumption has stronger evidence than the other. A precise model output is not proof of a precise economic forecast. Time: 2 minutes."
