      "body": "\n<div class=\"header-row\"><h2>Better ratios still need a value-creation test</h2></div><div class=\"rule\"></div><div class=\"investment-test\"><div><span>Closing arithmetic</span><strong>$200M capex</strong><p>Changes the asset mix immediately</p></div><div><span>Operating hurdle</span><strong>9% WACC</strong><p>$18M annual after-tax operating return at the hurdle</p></div><div><span>Decision evidence</span><strong>NPV &gt; 0</strong><p>Forecast timing, reinvestment, risk, and cash flows</p></div></div><div class=\"deliverable\">Workbook simplification: capex enters EV dollar-for-dollar at closing. Challenge that assumption before claiming value creation.</div>\n    ",
      "note": "The existing workbook assumes 100 percent immediate EV recognition for capex. Explain that this is a bookkeeping simplification, not evidence of positive NPV. Two hundred million at 9 percent implies an 18 million annual after-tax operating-return benchmark, but project value still depends on the full future cash-flow and reinvestment schedule. Compare incremental ROIC with WACC, not ROE with WACC. The 47.70 million net-income hurdle on the previous slide preserves EPS; it is not the project investment hurdle. Ask for one measurable operating milestone and one reason to reject the project. Time: 2 minutes."
